        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "30c45f57c99e328ec3e06187e8e2877ea2e46338",
            "parents": [
                "6aa9888495a4525cfa8e864f76f2317730422e0d"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/03/89603/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                "username": "BartoszJanicki"
            },
            "createdOn": 1777032330,
            "author": {
                "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                "username": "BartoszJanicki"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2401,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Bills",
                        "email": "jason.m.bills@linux.intel.com",
                        "username": "jmbills"
                    },
                    "message": "Is this just to avoid calling `contains()`?  The code is pretty simple either way, so I don't think readability is a concern, but I'm curious if the compiler optimizations would have already resolved a double-lookup with `contains()`."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2401,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                        "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                        "username": "BartoszJanicki"
                    },
                    "message": "with `contain()` + `operator[]` to assign value we perform double-lookup. \nWith iterator we perform only one lookup. \n\nI don't think compiler will optimize the first approach - even with `-O3`.\nSee: \nhttps://godbolt.org/z/dT9qTsGed"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2401,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Bills",
                        "email": "jason.m.bills@linux.intel.com",
                        "username": "jmbills"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2408,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Bills",
                        "email": "jason.m.bills@linux.intel.com",
                        "username": "jmbills"
                    },
                    "message": "If this is like `std::get`, it can throw an exception and terminate the program.  If that isn't what we want, we should use `std::get_if` to allow checking for error."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2408,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                        "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                        "username": "BartoszJanicki"
                    },
                    "message": "I was thinking about adding some method to validate the provided config (especially for dbus) but since it is load in runtime i don't see much value - if it's wrong the service will exit anyway..\n\nIn such case the user is responsible for providing the correct config data - but handling this gracefully rather than termination is better approach. Will change it, however isn't the existing solution will also fail in such case? \n\nUpdated: \nThe nlohmann json does not contain `get_if` - used `get_ptr`:\nhttps://json.nlohmann.me/api/basic_json/get_ptr/"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2408,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                        "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                        "username": "BartoszJanicki"
                    },
                    "message": "Verified with corrupted config file  - provided signalType as int in json. \nService did not terminated"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2408,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Bills",
                        "email": "jason.m.bills@linux.intel.com",
                        "username": "jmbills"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2431,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Bills",
                        "email": "jason.m.bills@linux.intel.com",
                        "username": "jmbills"
                    },
                    "message": "Same here.  We should use `std::get_if` to avoid a throw if that's what is being called here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/power_control.cpp",
                    "line": 2431,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Bartosz Janicki",
                        "email": "bartosz.janicki@intel.com",
                        "username": "BartoszJanicki"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
